<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.Views.Settings.BehaviorCard"
             FontSize="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}">
  <!-- ══ 行为 — four check boxes and two label-and-control rows on the DLU grid ═══════════════
       ROUND 8, TASK 6 REPLACED THE ROW GRAMMAR. See AppearanceCard.axaml's header for the whole
       argument, the published citations for 5 / 7 / 11 / 3, why the rhythm is a per-row Margin
       rather than Grid.RowSpacing, and why every card root states the FontSize above. This file is
       where two consequences of the grammar land, and it is also where the missing body size was
       most visible: the vendored CheckBox theme sets no FontSize, so the four boxes below and the
       固定到任务栏 label all drew at 14 while the button beside that label drew at the Aero Button
       theme's 12.

       ── THE CHECK BOXES CARRY THEIR OWN LABELS NOW ────────────────────────────────────────────
       Round 8, Task 5 turned four ToggleSwitches into CheckBoxes and said, in this very comment
       block, that the label would move ONTO the box in Task 6 "together with the row grammar it
       belongs to". This is that move. PUBLISHED, ctrl-check-boxes: a check box's label is its
       Content, and a row whose only control is a check box is not a label-and-control pair — it is
       one self-labelling control. So each of the four spans both columns and starts on the label
       column's own x, and the four AutomationProperties.LabeledBy attributes Task 5 added are gone
       with the four title TextBlocks they pointed at: a CheckBox with Content has an accessible name
       of its own (ContentControlAutomationPeer's presenter/Content fallback), which is what Task 5's
       LabeledBy was standing in for while the label was still a separate element.

       Their four subtitles are deleted with them, and so are AppLanguage's AutoCloseCardSub,
       TopMostCardSub, AutoStartCardSub and HotkeyEnabledCardSub: every one of them restated its own
       label ("窗口失去焦点时自动隐藏" under 失焦自动隐藏). The two lines that are NOT restatements stay,
       as static text under the control they describe — AutoStartHelp, which explains why the box
       above it is disabled on a portable build, and HotkeyHelpText, which explains how to work the
       capture box beside it.

       ── THE TWO REMAINING PAIRS ARE REAL ROWS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
       固定到任务栏 and 热键 have a label in column 0 and their control in column 1, and each has a
       line below it that belongs to the row rather than to the grid: the live pin state (eight
       AppLanguage strings, five in ApplyTaskbarPinState's switch and three in the click handlers)
       and the hotkey help. Both span both columns, 3 below their control.

       NONE OF THE EIGHT PIN STRINGS MAY CONTAIN A HARD "\n", and that constraint SURVIVES the row
       grammar it was written for even though its old reason does not. It used to be invariant I1 —
       a break took the header past two lines and moved the row's icon and control off the y every
       other row centred on. There is no icon and no two-line header now; what a break costs today is
       that the status line pushes 启用全局热键 and everything below it down by a whole line at every
       window width, on a dialog whose height is a literal. PinToTaskbarFailed carries the comment
       saying why it may not, and SettingsRowTests guards the labels.

       ── WHAT TASK 5 MEASURED, KEPT BECAUSE IT IS STILL THE CHECK BOX'S GEOMETRY ────────────────
       Measured on this machine at 150 % on a build with Task 5 and a build without — UIA
       BoundingRectangle for the bounds, a PrintWindow row scan across each control's centre for the
       ink — on all four rows, when the boxes were still contentless and still trailing:

         element bounds   102 x 51 physical  ->  20 x 20   (68 x 34 DIP -> 13.33 x 13.33)
         element RIGHT    x = 4365           ->  x = 4347
         last INK column  x = 4346           ->  x = 4346  (UNCHANGED)

       That is why Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml's ToggleSwitch.tm-card-toggle could be deleted without
       moving a pixel: the toggle only reached that column by overhanging its own bounds by 12 DIP of
       empty presenter. The numbers describe a trailing, contentless box and are NOT re-asserted for
       the labelled, left-aligned boxes below — this task moved them. -->
  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto"
        ColumnSpacing="5" Margin="11">

    <CheckBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" x:Name="AutoCloseToggle"
              IsCheckedChanged="OnAutoCloseChanged" />

    <CheckBox Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" x:Name="TopMostToggle"
              Margin="0,7,0,0" IsCheckedChanged="OnTopMostChanged" />

    <CheckBox Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" x:Name="AutoStartToggle"
              Margin="0,7,0,0" IsCheckedChanged="OnAutoStartChanged" />
    <!-- Portable builds only, and IsVisible is set from the code-behind rather than bound: this line
         says why the box above it is disabled, and there is nothing to say when it is not. A
         collapsed child contributes neither height nor margin, so the 7 below still measures from
         the check box. -->
    <TextBlock Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" x:Name="AutoStartHelp"
               TextWrapping="Wrap" IsVisible="False" Margin="0,3,0,0" />

    <TextBlock Grid.Row="4" Grid.Column="0" x:Name="PinTaskbarLabel" VerticalAlignment="Center"
               Margin="0,7,0,0" />
    <!-- The only button on this row. Its Theme is now STATIC, and Round 7, Task 3 is what made it
         so: TmAccentButton was deleted with the arrival of the Aero control themes (Windows 7 had
         no accent-filled default button), and the run-time Theme swap in ApplyTaskbarPinState went
         with it, because both of its branches had become TmButton.

         What the swap existed for still holds, and is still delivered — by IsEnabled alone. Round
         3, Task 3 (user item 8): once pinned there is no affirmative action left to offer, only a
         completed state to report, and a disabled TmAccentButton still painted a dimmed BLUE
         swatch (its ^:disabled rule only lowers Opacity; it never repainted Background off
         AccentDefaultBrush), which read as a live call to action, just quieter. A disabled
         TmButton has no accent fill to dim, so "done" now falls out of IsEnabled="false" — set by
         ApplyTaskbarPinState from the live COM pin query — instead of out of a second theme.

         The static Theme is also what makes the first frame of a process defensible.
         OptionsWindow's constructor calls ReloadFromConfig (hence ApplyTaskbarPinState) through
         ApplyText while this card tree is still detached from the logical tree — a TabControl only
         realises the selected tab's content and this card sits on the second tab — and there
         FindResource cannot reach Application.Styles at all; a Button with no Theme and no Classes
         would have rendered as stock Fluent for that frame. -->
    <!-- NO AutomationProperties.LabeledBy here, unlike 外观's four controls and unlike HotkeyBox
         below, and that is deliberate: this button's Content is already its own name and it is the
         MORE informative one — ApplyTaskbarPinState rewrites it between 固定到任务栏 / 打开固定位置 /
         已固定到任务栏 as the live COM query moves. A LabeledBy would win over the content fallback
         in ControlAutomationPeer.GetNameCore and freeze the name at the static label beside it. -->
    <Button Grid.Row="4" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="PinTaskbarBtn" Theme="{StaticResource TmButton}"
            HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,7,0,0"
            Click="OnPinTaskbarClick" />
    <TextBlock Grid.Row="5" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" x:Name="PinTaskbarStatusText"
               TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,3,0,0" />

    <CheckBox Grid.Row="6" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" x:Name="HotkeyEnabledToggle"
              Margin="0,7,0,0" IsCheckedChanged="OnHotkeyEnabledChanged" />

    <TextBlock Grid.Row="7" Grid.Column="0" x:Name="HotkeyLabel" VerticalAlignment="Center"
               Margin="0,7,0,0" />
    <StackPanel Grid.Row="7" Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="7"
                VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,7,0,0">
      <!-- Key capture is wired in code with a TUNNELING KeyDown handler (see the ctor): a
           bubbling handler would run after the TextBox has already consumed the key.

           tm-keycap carries only this box's SIZE and PADDING; its paint is the theme's. It was
           once the ONE control on the whole settings surface with no Classes and no Theme, so in
           every state it fell through to the stock text box — Fluent's then — whose disabled fill
           was a different grey from the one every disabled button beside it used, i.e. two disabled
           vocabularies on one surface. Four TextBox.tm-keycap /template/ rules in
           Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml existed to fix exactly that, and Round 7 Task 7 DELETED all
           four: every control here is a vendored Aero theme now, so "one disabled vocabulary" is
           true by construction, and re-pointing the rules would have repainted a part with the
           value it already had. The box shows Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/TextBox.axaml's own four
           states, and the focus signal that says "I am listening" changed hands from a hand-painted
           accent hairline to Aero's own focus border over its focus fill. -->
      <TextBox x:Name="HotkeyBox" Classes="tm-keycap" IsReadOnly="True"
               AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding #HotkeyLabel}" />
      <!-- Clearing a hotkey is a reset, not a forward-moving action, so this button never
           qualified for the accent treatment even though it is the row's only button. The
           criterion is moot now: Round 7, Task 3 deleted TmAccentButton along with the rest of
           the accent-filled default button (Windows 7 had none), leaving TmButton the only
           settings button theme there is. Kept because it records WHY this row never changed when
           the two buttons that did carry the accent were moved. -->
      <Button x:Name="HotkeyClearBtn" Theme="{StaticResource TmButton}" Click="OnHotkeyClearClick" />
    </StackPanel>
    <!-- 右侧 / "on the right" is still the truth this line tells: HotkeyBox sits in column 1, beside
         the 热键 label, not above this sentence. AppLanguage's HotkeyHelp was reworded from 上方 to
         右侧 when Round 5 moved the box; Task 6 did not move it back. -->
    <TextBlock Grid.Row="8" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" x:Name="HotkeyHelpText"
               TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,3,0,0" />
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
